rzmq 0.5.26: zero-copy io_uring receive for large frames, and a real SENDMSG_ZC send path.

Receive (IO_URING_RCV_DIRECT_THRESHOLD, default 32 KiB, 0 = off):
  With multishot receive, every received byte was copied from a provided-buffer ring slot
  into the engine's accumulator (ZmtpEngine::on_network_bytes -> extend_from_slice), and
  the accumulator was regrown (realloc/copy) while a large frame built up: ~1.7x the
  payload in userspace memcpy per received byte at 100 KiB-1 MiB. Once a connection sees a
  data frame with at least the threshold still to arrive, it retires the multishot op and
  reads with single-shot IORING_OP_RECV straight into the engine buffer; the rest of a
  large frame is received with MSG_WAITALL into a buffer sized for the whole frame, which
  the parser slices into the Msg without copying (libzmq-style). While large messages
  flow, each next message is read whole into a buffer sized like the last one (one
  receive per message for same-shaped streams). Bounded: at most 16 MiB
  is allocated ahead of data actually arriving, so a header announcing 2^44 bytes cannot
  force a huge allocation. A buffer the kernel may still write into is owned by its op and
  leaked rather than freed if the op is torn down without its CQE; on close, in-flight
  receives are detached from the fd (which can be reused at once) and cancelled.
  Only for framers whose wire bytes are plain ZMTP (NULL, PLAIN); CURVE/Noise unchanged.

Send (IO_URING_SNDZEROCOPY):
  Data never reached SEND_ZC: the io_uring handler sends every batch with WRITEV, and
  the existing SEND_ZC path (unused for data) first copies into a registered buffer. Large
  vectored batches now go out as IORING_OP_SENDMSG_ZC directly from the message Bytes;
  the payload stays tracked under the same user_data until the IORING_CQE_F_NOTIF
  notification (so the id is not recycled early). With IORING_SEND_ZC_REPORT_USAGE, a
  connection whose sends the kernel keeps reporting as copied (loopback, veth, NICs
  without SG/csum offload) switches back to writev after 64 in a row, as the Linux
  MSG_ZEROCOPY docs recommend.

Tests: core/tests/io_uring_direct_recv.rs.

